<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-chapter-11" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Big Theorems of Vector Calculus</title>

<introduction>
  <p>
    In this final chapter, we study two major results that unify many of the ideas developed
    throughout vector calculus. Stokes’ Theorem and the Divergence Theorem connect integrals over
    regions to integrals over their boundaries, revealing deep relationships between local behavior
    and global outcomes in vector fields.
  </p>

  <p>
    We begin with Stokes’ Theorem, which links surface integrals of curl to line integrals around
    boundary curves. Orientation and the right-hand rule play a central role, and careful geometric
    reasoning helps ensure correct setup. This theorem generalizes Green’s Theorem and extends its
    ideas into three dimensions.
  </p>

  <p>
    The chapter concludes with the Divergence Theorem, which relates flux through a closed surface
    to divergence within the enclosed region. By the end of the chapter, you should be able to see
    Stokes’ Theorem and the Divergence Theorem as natural culminations of earlier concepts, tying
    together line integrals, surface integrals, and volume integrals into a coherent framework.
  </p>
</introduction>



